  <!-- ===== Hero ===== -->
  <section class="hero">
    <h1>Danica Abella</h1>
    <p class="role">Operations and Client Support <span>|</span> Subscription Operations <span>|</span> Process Documentation</p>

    <p>Eight years, five companies, one constant: I'm the person clients reach when something's wrong with their account, and the person the team escalates to when the first answer didn't work.</p>

    <p>Broken bookings and post-stay complaints in travel, at-risk accounts in telecom, complex customer cases beyond an agent's scope, and most recently subscriptions and invoices in hospitality SaaS. I get it corrected in the system, then write down how, so the next person doesn't have to work it out again.</p>

    <p class="signature">Steady support. Clean records. Processes that outlast the person who built them.</p>

    <div class="ledger">
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Experience</div><div class="ledger-val">8+ years in customer support and operations</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Industries</div><div class="ledger-val">Telecom, travel, finance, and hospitality SaaS</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Core skills</div><div class="ledger-val">Customer support, subscriptions and billing, collections, CRM, admin support, and documentation</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Track record</div><div class="ledger-val">Consistently top-scoring across every role, with formal recognition at 4 companies, including Employee of the Month in my most recent role</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Availability</div><div class="ledger-val">Around 20 hours per week, with daily overlap for US Eastern afternoons and US Pacific mornings, exact hours by arrangement</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Handover</div><div class="ledger-val">Everything I run gets documented, so it survives handover</div></div>
      <div class="ledger-row"><div class="ledger-key">Location</div><div class="ledger-val">Fully remote, based in the Philippines, PHT (GMT+8)</div></div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== How I Can Help ===== -->
  <section id="help">
    <h2>How I Can Help</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">I work as an embedded part of your team, not a project consultant. You get a dependable point of contact for a defined set of operations, and written documentation of everything I run, so nothing lives only in my head. I take on a set number of hours each week, currently around 20, agreed with you at the start so the scope matches the time.</p>

    <div class="services">
      <div class="service">
        <h3>Customer Support Coverage</h3>
        <p>Email and chat worked daily: triage, respond, and resolve. Complex cases investigated before escalating, and recurring issues flagged so they get fixed instead of re-answered.</p>
        <p class="fit"><b>Good fit if:</b> your queue needs consistent daily attention rather than full-time cover.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service">
        <h3>Subscription and Billing Records</h3>
        <p>Subscriptions created and maintained as deals close, payments matched against records, invoice adjustments and credit notes, and contract-to-billing mismatches caught early.</p>
        <p class="fit"><b>Good fit if:</b> subscriptions are being set up by whoever's free, or you've found invoices that don't match the contract.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service">
        <h3>Collections and Payment Follow-Up</h3>
        <p>Overdue invoice follow-up that is tracked rather than ad hoc, payment arrangements agreed and documented, and invoice corrections that unblock a stalled payment.</p>
        <p class="fit"><b>Good fit if:</b> overdue invoices are chased when someone remembers, or nobody owns following them up.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service">
        <h3>CRM and Data Hygiene</h3>
        <p>Ongoing data entry and cleanup against clear standards, duplicates and gaps caught, recurring reports that stay current, and filing systems that stay organized.</p>
        <p class="fit"><b>Good fit if:</b> your reports don't reflect reality, or nobody can find last quarter's documents.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service">
        <h3>Admin and Executive Support</h3>
        <p>Calendar, inbox, and scheduling support, document preparation and templates, task tracking, and recurring operational work owned end to end.</p>
        <p class="fit"><b>Good fit if:</b> your leadership time is going to logistics instead of decisions.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="service">
        <h3>Documentation: Included, Not Extra</h3>
        <p>Every engagement produces written process documentation: SOPs, help center articles, or internal guides, depending on what's useful. It's how the work survives holidays, turnover, and growth.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <!-- ===== Work Samples ===== -->
  <section id="samples">
    <h2>Work Samples</h2>
    <p class="standfirst">Six pieces of work showing how I approach operations problems: finding what's wrong in financial and customer data, cleaning up records, and writing down the processes that keep it from happening again.</p>

    <div class="notice">
      <strong>All samples use fabricated data.</strong> I don't publish client work, even anonymized, so every company name, figure, and record below is invented. The structure, formulas, and reasoning are the same as I'd apply to real work.
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">1</div>
      <h3>Billing and Revenue Audits</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">Invoices and signed contracts quietly drifted apart over a year. The billing system configuration looked correct, but individual invoices went out wrong.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What I did</div><div class="entry-val">Audited 33 issued invoices line by line against contract terms, recalculating prorations, verifying price-change timing, and checking percentage-based charges.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What it found</div><div class="entry-val"><span class="pen">10 discrepancies, USD 2,430.73 net recoverable.</span> Identified 8 uncollected revenue issues including unapplied escalation clauses and unbilled booking overages.</div></div>
      <p class="note"><b>Worth noting:</b> the most expensive errors are definitional, for example a gross rather than net commission basis. Automated checks never catch that; only reading agreements against invoices does.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">2</div>
      <h3>Subscription Reconciliation</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">Monthly recurring revenue reports and contract records disagreed, and nobody had cross-checked them.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What I did</div><div class="entry-val">Built a formula-driven workbook matching 37 subscription records against contracts on price, frequency, currency, dates, and discounts.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What it found</div><div class="entry-val"><span class="pen">19 records flagged</span> across 8 discrepancy types, including active subscriptions with no contract and signed contracts never billed.</div></div>
      <p class="note"><b>Worth noting:</b> billing without a contract is a refund risk; a signed unbilled contract is uncollected revenue.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">3</div>
      <h3>CRM Cleanup (for Operations Teams)</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">Fragmented CRM data: duplicate companies, missing job titles, and inconsistent country names such as "UK", "GB", and "United Kingdom".</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What I did</div><div class="entry-val">Profiled records, corrected data, produced an import-ready file with original values preserved, and built a full audit change log.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What it found</div><div class="entry-val"><span class="pen">39 field-level edits across 15 records</span> and 6 duplicate groups. Flagged plain-text credentials for escalation.</div></div>
      <p class="note"><b>Worth noting:</b> I never delete records. Duplicates are proposed for merge with client review before committing.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">4</div>
      <h3>Knowledge Base Restructure</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">34 pages in a flat list, with multiple conflicting versions of the same procedure, so staff asked in chat instead of searching.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What I did</div><div class="entry-val">Audited every page as keep, rewrite, or retire, restructured around the questions people actually ask, and rewrote core procedures with named owners and review dates.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What it found</div><div class="entry-val"><span class="pen">9 duplicates resolved, 12 pages archived with notes</span>, and plain-text credentials escalated for urgent removal.</div></div>
      <p class="note"><b>Worth noting:</b> documentation usually fails on missing thresholds, for example an approval limit nobody wrote down, not on writing quality.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">5</div>
      <h3>Knowledge Base Built From Scratch</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">A company running on informal knowledge, with an abandoned Notion workspace nobody used.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What I did</div><div class="entry-val">Built a three-level Notion structure organized by task, completed an ownership map, and populated the critical operational procedures.</div></div>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">What it found</div><div class="entry-val"><span class="pen">3 of 10 mapped areas had no owner at all</span>: software renewals, equipment repairs, and client data requests.</div></div>
      <p class="note"><b>Worth noting:</b> an ownership map reveals operational gaps an organization didn't know it had.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="sample">
      <div class="sample-n">6</div>
      <h3>Process Documentation</h3>
      <div class="entry"><div class="entry-key">The problem</div><div class="entry-val">Bug escalations depended entirely on one team member's knowledge, and stalled whenever they were away.</div></div>
